  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAfter a gorgeous day which will feature a mix of \nsun and clouds with highs in the 50s/60s, our weather will turn \nactive across eastern New York and western New England to close out \nthe weekend.\n\nTo our south, a stalled frontal boundary is located from central \nIowa into the southern Ohio River Valley and Carolinas. As southerly \nflow strengthens tonight with the formation of a surface low across \nthe mid-Mississippi River Valley, this boundary will quickly move \nnorth as a warm front into the Mid-Atlantic, aided by a 30-40 kt 925-\n850 hPa LLJ. This jet will strengthen moisture transport into the \nMid-Atlantic and southern New England, with PWATs climbing to \naround 1.50-1.75\" in these area per the NBM. The combination of \nthis moisture and lift provided by the front and trailing \nsurface low will result in a light to moderate stratiform rain \nfor the region for much of the daytime, with activity expected \nto wane by evening as the low departs off the coast of Cape Cod.\nExpected rainfall hasn't deviated much from the previous \nforecast, with amounts ranging from about half an inch across\nthe western ADKs, to around 0.75-1.5\" along and south of the\nInterstate 90 corridor. Locally higher amounts will be possible\nacross the higher terrain of the Catskills and the Interstate 84\ncorridor. Daytime temperatures will struggle to hit 60 across \nthe high terrain and areas north of Interstate 90, though low to\nmid 60s are expected for valley locations south of the Albany \nmetro.\n\nThinking regarding hydro concerns remains unchanged from the \nprevious AFD. Given the lack of available instability, stratiform \nnature of the rain and high FFG values, flash flooding is not \nexpected Sunday outside of nuisance ponding of water in low-lying, \nurban, and poor drainage areas. This rain will actually be \nbeneficial for most, especially across the Mid Hudson Valley, \nwestern New England and Mohawk Valley where dry conditions have \nprevailed with below normal precipitation in the last 1-2 months. \nRiver flooding probabilities are also low with HEFS and National \nWater Model ensembles maintaining river level forecasts below action \nstage.\n\nHigh pressure will follow behind the low and a cold front for early \nnext week, which will result in a pleasant, dry start to Fall across \nthe region. There do remain some low chances (10-30%) of rain \nshowers mainly across Ulster and Dutchess counties early Tuesday \nwith a passing surface low across the Ohio River Valley, but most \nwill remain dry. Temperatures each day will range from highs in the \n50s (terrain) to 60s (valleys) and lows in the 30s (terrain) to 40s \n(valleys).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nLatest guidance continues to hint at potential for \na coastal system to impact the region late in the forecast period. \nThe GFS remains the most aggressive in bringing a strong upper level \ntrough into the Appalachians and Virginia coast, with development \ninto a cutoff low off the Mid-Atlantic coast early Friday. Given \nhigh levels of moisture expected to accompany the system, we will \ncontinue to monitor its placement and trends in the coming\nforecasts.",
